bianbu-agent-runtime (0.1.155) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Keep the Session transcript header in a zstd frame of its own. Harness's
    reader requires the first frame to be exactly one header line and refuses
    anything else as corrupt, but the transcript compressed the header together
    with the first append batch. The first append now writes the header frame
    and the batch frame back to back inside one temporary file that is linked
    into place, so the atomic commit and the idempotent retry byte comparison
    stay as they were and a metadata-only reader only decodes frame one.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Sat, 10 Oct 2026 22:00:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-runtime (0.1.154) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Write the transcript in the three row shapes DeepSeek Harness uses: one
    immutable header row, one row per event, and packed `text-chunks` /
    `reasoning-chunks` rows that stand for a whole run of streaming deltas.
    Packing rebuilds `seq = seq0 + i` and accumulates `dt`, so the logical log
    stays exact while a token-per-row message collapses to one row; a run of
    fewer than three deltas, or non-monotonic times, stays one row per event.
    An unknown event or packed row is now rejected unless it declares
    `ignorable`, instead of silently dropping an event this build cannot
    rebuild, and `tool-call-chunks` is deliberately not written because a tool
    call arrives whole and has no losslessly sliceable text stream.
  * Drop the retired Session mounts from the Worker policy. Removing the
    checkpointer deleted `sessions/checkpoints`, but an un-upgraded securityd
    still declared it as a writable Worker mount and the Runtime refuses to
    spawn a Worker whose policy names a path that does not exist, so every Run
    failed with `worker_rpc_error`. The control plane owns the Session log and
    a Worker only reads it through the read-only data root, so every
    `sessions/` mount is dropped while the Worker policy is augmented.

