bianbu-agent-runtime (0.1.142) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Stop folding every Session transcript at startup. A start rebuilt the whole
    Run listing by decompressing and folding every transcript: on K3, 175
    Sessions and 1.14M events held the API back for 63.7s, and listing one
    Session re-read its entire log. Session projections now live in a persisted
    row table (one file, replaced atomically, written fail-soft); `open()` reads
    only that table, a Session is folded on first touch, and a row whose
    recorded byte size still matches its transcript proves no newer frame
    exists, so listing, tail reads and startup recovery answer from the row
    without folding.
  * Keep the transcript the authority. A row is a fold shortcut, never a truth:
    a Session's first touch drops what the row seeded and refolds, a row is only
    usable when every unit it carries is readable at its live version, and an
    unusable or missing table falls back to the log instead of failing a start.
  * Wake startup recovery only for Sessions the rows already know, so a lost
    table cannot turn into a fold of every transcript again, and ask each Agent
    id at most once per recovery pass. Agent declarations are read and parsed
    again on every lookup and recovery asked once per Session: 179 parses, 22.1s
    of YAML on K3.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Tue, 29 Sep 2026 17:00:15 +0800

bianbu-agent-runtime (0.1.141) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Publish an MCP mutation without holding the catalog lock. Installing an
    MCP server held `runtime.catalog_mutation_lock` while it awaited the
    Worker reload, and the Worker answers a reload only after it has rebuilt
    the affected packages. The first load of an `npx` server materialises its
    npm package first, which takes minutes on a board, so the install request
    never returned and the lock stayed taken: the added server never appeared
    in any list and every later MCP install, delete or health check queued
    behind it. The lock now only covers the catalog and filesystem decision;
    the change is published to the Worker outside the lock, with a bounded
    wait, and the mutation answers from the catalog it owns. A busy Worker can
    no longer freeze the API, and a server registered by name and command is
    visible in the MCP list immediately.
  * Keep the failure semantics unchanged, and report the new
    `mcp_install_not_visible` when a staged install is somehow missing from
    the catalog instead of answering 201 for a server nobody can see.

