bianbu-agent-runtime (0.1.125) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * One system-level Runtime per device. The per-user template unit
    bianbu-agent-runtime@.service is replaced by bianbu-agent-runtime.service,
    wanted by multi-user.target, so the Runtime starts at boot and no longer
    depends on who logs in. postinst resolves the device user, records it in
    /etc/bianbu-agent-runtime/service-user and drops in
    /etc/systemd/system/bianbu-agent-runtime.service.d/10-service-user.conf to
    override User= and BIANBU_RUNTIME_SERVICE_USER=; an image build that sees no
    device user keeps the packaged bianbu-runtime default and simply does not
    enable the unit. polkit matches the control plane by
    subject.system_unit == bianbu-agent-runtime.service instead of decoding a
    user name out of the instance name, and local active users may start or
    restart the unit so the start-menu entry can open the console after boot.
  * No desktop icon any more. The package stops copying an icon into ~/Desktop;
    the /usr/share/applications entry stays, and prerm removes the copy an older
    version left behind only when it is byte-identical to the packaged template.
  * Callers can see whose Runtime they are talking to. /api/v1/runtime/status
    reports service_user and data_dir, the console "Runtime status" panel and
    the desktop launcher window show the run identity and data directory, and
    debian/bianbu-agent-wrapper resolves the service account's
    ~/.bianbu-agent/config.yaml (explicit BIANBU_AGENT_CONFIG still wins, a
    source checkout without the record file still uses the login user). The
    launcher falls back to the uid-derived console port when it cannot read the
    service configuration.
  * Console: an already configured custom-provider API key now shows
    "Configured - enter a new value to replace" as its placeholder and an empty
    field keeps the stored key; the MCP page lists standalone servers only and
    points at the Plugins page, where each plugin's own MCP servers are listed
    with live status and tool count (unknown when no snapshot is available).
  * The starter expert selects every MCP a plugin provides ('*') instead of
    listing them one by one.

